server: context on demand - the model stays loaded, a request gives its context back

A loaded model holds its weights and its context: the KV cache and the
compute buffers, sized for the window and the batch whether or not a request
runs. With --context-on-demand the server keeps the weights and lets its
clients give the context back between their requests. A request that carries
`keep_context_ms` has the context freed that many milliseconds after its
answer (0: at once), and the next request that needs one creates it again;
requests back to back carry a keep that bridges the time between them and
pay one creation for the run. A request that does not carry it uses the
context as it always did - it is not freed after it, cached prompt included -
so a client that does not know the option is served as by a server without
it. It is the `keep_alive` of other runners applied to the context instead of
the whole model, in milliseconds. Several servers on one device then need
room only for the contexts that work at the same time.

- common: common_init_result::free_context() / init_context() and
  common_init_context_from_params(): the context is created again from the
  context params of the first creation (no fitting again), then the steps
  common_init_from_params() runs after a creation, in the order they have
  there (control vectors, the check of a reranking model, lora adapters,
  warmup) - that part is now common_init_context_apply(), shared by both.
  The load returns a context one of these steps stopped on, as before, and
  records where (common_init_result::init_stop); a later creation is
  accepted when its steps end in the same place - the state of the load's
  context, or none - so a model the load serves is not refused afterwards.
- server_queue::on_idle(): a callback run by the start_loop() thread each
  time it finds no task, without the queue's lock (it may free the
  context, and tasks are posted meanwhile); it returns when it wants to be
  called again, and the wait ends then instead of at the next one-second
  check.
- server_context: only the start_loop() thread creates and frees the
  context. With the option on, process_single_task() creates it for a task
  that needs one (context_acquire); a creation that fails is answered with 503
  `unavailable_error` and the server stays up, where a failed reload after
  --sleep-idle-seconds aborts. A slot's release reads its task's keep
  (context_keep): none, and the context is held; one, and the deadline is
  extended to it - the latest asked wins, a request never shortens
  another's. The idle callback frees the context once the deadline has
  passed and nothing holds it (context_idle, context_release). The context
  of the load itself goes once the server is idle. The slots drop their
  cached prompt with the KV cache. get_meta(), n_ctx_slot() and
  use_mixed_batch() no longer need a context, so /props, /models, /metrics,
  /slots and the tokenizer routes neither wait for one nor create one.
- --keep-context-ms N gives a keep to the requests that carry none, for a
  server whose clients cannot be changed (default: -1, they leave the
  context loaded).
- /metrics: context_loaded, context_creations_total,
  context_creation_seconds_total.
- a task's results say when the context was created for it, and how long
  that took (server_task_result::t_ctx_create_us, negative when the context
  was there): the server's own word per request, for a client that counts
  creations - it cannot tell one from its own clock, since the context may
  have been kept for another client's request. /embedding's results carry
  it as `context_create_ms`.
- the context canary (on by default with the option, --no-context-canary):
  a context created on demand is new memory and a new first pass, so the
  load records the output its context gives for a fixed short input - the
  pooled embedding with --embedding, the logits of the last token otherwise,
  computed twice, the load refused if the two differ - and every context
  created later computes the same input before any task runs on it. One
  that does not give the same values, bit for bit, is discarded and its
  request answered 503 `unavailable_error` ("context canary failed: ...",
  retryable). After the lora adapters are set the next context records it
  again. /metrics: context_canary, context_canary_failures_total; the
  creation's time includes the canary's pass.
- refused at load with speculative decoding or a multimodal projector, whose
  contexts are bound to the model's.

Checked on the CPU (random-weight llama and qwen3 models written by
tests/test-llama-archs -o): tools/server/tests/unit/test_context_on_demand.py
- the load's context freed when idle and none created by the read-only
routes; a request with a keep of 0 creates the context and frees it; a
request without a keep leaves it loaded, cached prompt included, until one
that carries a keep is answered; a keep from the request (completion and chat
completion) and from the server; a keep of 0 that does not shorten another;
the same completion and the same embedding, value for value, across
creations; the cached prompt reused within a keep and gone after it; a
creation refused with 503, then served; eight concurrent requests; /slots
listed without a context; together with --sleep-idle-seconds; a result says
when the context was created for it, one result of a batch, none without the
option; each created context held to the canary (completion and embedding
models); a context made to fail its canary is discarded, its request refused
with 503 and no context left behind, and the next one serves; the canary
switched off; no canary without the option; the same rerank scores across
contexts (for a model whose load stops at the reranking check, which failed
before init_stop). With the option
off the existing suites give the same results as before the patch
(test_sleep, test_basic, test_metrics, test_slot_save, test_embedding,
test_completion, test_tokenize, test_ctx_shift, test_kv_keep_only_active).
